{
  "_comment": "The format's good and bad cases, run by BOTH scripts/validate.py --self-test and meshTerm's HostRecipesTests (a byte-identical copy in its test fixtures), so the Python and Swift rules agree case by case. templates: a run text (in `intent` when given) is ok, unusable (a later app's placeholder: the variant is skipped, the file accepted) or refused (the whole file). variants: a whole variant object, same three results. renders: the text for the given values (null: not rendered). selection: which run the app picks for an OS (null: none), with the bundled document's variants as the fallback for an unusable winner; `idLike` / `bundledIdLike` are the two documents' top-level allowlists (absent: none), `otherIds` the ids another intent of the document names, `classifierIds` the ids the app's classifier names, `os.idLike` the host's ID_LIKE, `os.familyInherited` that the caller took the family from the parent, and `parent` the parent resolved from the document in use (and passed unchanged to the bundled fallback). documents: a top-level `idLike` value is ok or refused (the whole file). familyRoots: the ID_LIKE family roots both resolvers hold (recipes_lib.ID_LIKE_FAMILY_ROOTS, HostRecipeDocument.idLikeFamilyRoots): walking ID_LIKE in order, an allowlisted token is the parent, a root off the allowlist stops the walk (no parent), any other token (a named derivative or an unrecognised id) is skipped.",
